  "declaration_audit_decision": "Current Clone implementations already represent copy construction and resized Assign construction, with clone_from representing assignment. Adding constructor callbacks alone would duplicate represented type operations; native throwing-copy allocation/prefix cleanup remains an explicit different domain. Init from an internal source reference is instead a native-defined alias protocol missing from the external &mut self / &T interfaces, so this slice supplies an indexed adapter.",
  "changes": [
    "Add two init_from_index_with_assignment adapters for native Init with its donor selected from the same array.",
    "Split storage into a disjoint prefix, donor and suffix. Invoke ordinary assignment in prefix order, exactly one self-assignment callback, then ordinary assignment in suffix order while reading the live donor.",
    "Observe donor mutations at the self step in every later assignment. Retain native partial state on failures before, during or after self assignment, including mutations made before an exception.",
    "Validate the source indices before callbacks and preserve target bounds and storage. Matrix traversal uses its inherited flat extent while its donor is selected by row/column.",
    "Keep the entire engine safe Rust with no raw alias construction, Clone or Default requirement; caller-supplied FnOnce self-assignment supports nontrivial self behavior."
  ],
  "probe_policy": "The native payload deletes copy construction, detects this == &donor in operator=, records Q ordinary assignments and S self assignment, and adds 100 to the donor at the self step. Thirty-two rows cover both dimensions, owned/borrowed storage, first/middle/last sources and six success/failure modes. Three invalid-index rows, two empty arrays and two valid mismatched flat extents complete 39 rows consumed by eleven tests. Three Rust-only tests cover non-Default elements/FnOnce ownership and checked rejection outside shortened matrix backing.",
  "limitations": [
    "This is a domain adapter for the native Init(Value(...)) expression, not a newly discovered native overload. Value argument-validation dependencies receive no separate mapping credit.",
    "The callback pair explicitly supplies distinct-target and self-assignment behavior; no automatic inference of native operator=, self-assignment guarantees or overload selection is claimed.",
    "The supported alias is one live donor element inside a single exclusive array view. Arbitrary overlapping raw wrappers, aliases into subobjects, callback reentry and retained pointers across exclusive borrows remain outside the claim.",
    "Shared read-only views retain detachment-on-mutation semantics; native const-cast writable aliases remain unrepresented. The observed owned and mutable-borrowed domains preserve their storage and require no element construction.",
    "Native Array2 Value directly indexes Data after rectangle validation. Selecting a donor outside an independently shortened inherited buffer is undefined natively; Rust rejects it safely and that test earns no native parity credit.",
    "Rust panic/native exception comparisons cover defined assignment order, source/target state and lifetime events. Throwing destructors, native constructor-failure leaks, allocation hooks and uninitialized storage remain open domains.",
    "Original assertions and all prior array policies remain covered by cumulative tests and strict Miri. Neither class is complete; global API inventory and legacy-test mapping credit are unchanged."
  ],
